 \begin{abstract}
-Machine learning models for clinical prediction are often trained on small datasets, in which the number of records is too low for the complexity of the task, for example because the disease is rare or because labelled data are expensive to collect. Synthetic data generation can enlarge such datasets, but the usual generators, such as SMOTE, generative adversarial networks and variational autoencoders, learn only from the few records available and can therefore only recombine the patterns those records already contain. Large language models (LLMs) are an alternative because they need no training on the target dataset and bring general knowledge acquired during pre-training. Their output, however, is unreliable: when a commercial LLM was asked directly for a fixed number of records of six medical datasets, it never returned the requested number, delivering on average 88\% of it, and ten of its responses could not be read as records at all. This paper presents \system, a system that places a control layer between the LLM and the synthetic dataset. The control layer selects the real records shown to the model, validates every response against the dataset schema, corrects invalid records, requests the missing ones until the requested size is reached, and records the cost of each step. In experiments with four LLMs, ten classifiers and three established generators, \system returned datasets of the requested size, with 99.7\% to 100\% of their values filled, at a cost only 1.9\% higher than direct prompting. Adding its synthetic records to the training data increased the average F1-score of the classifiers by 1.1 percentage points with the strongest LLM and never decreased it by more than 0.6 points on any dataset, whereas the established generators decreased it by up to 10.6 points. These results show that LLMs can serve as a reliable and comparatively safe source of synthetic data for small datasets, provided that their output is controlled.
+Prediction models in healthcare are often trained on small datasets, in which the number of records is too low for the complexity of the task, for example because a disease is rare or because labelling records is expensive. Synthetic data generation can be a solution for such contexts, but established generators, such as oversampling techniques and generative adversarial networks, learn only from the available records and can therefore only reproduce patterns that already exist in the data. Large language models (LLMs) can go beyond these patterns, because they bring domain knowledge acquired during pre-training and do not need to be trained on the target data. When prompted directly, however, LLMs often return fewer records than requested, malformed records or values of the wrong type, so their output cannot be used as a dataset without further processing. To address this issue, we present \system, a system that places a control layer between the LLM and the synthetic dataset. The control layer infers the names and types of the columns from the real data, checks every response of the LLM against them, asks the model to correct the values that violate them, and keeps requesting records until the number set by the user is reached, while recording the cost of every request. \system was evaluated on six public healthcare datasets with four LLMs, three established generators and ten classifiers. It completed every synthetic dataset to the requested number of records, which direct prompting of the same LLM never achieved, at a cost only 1.9\% higher. With the strongest LLM, adding its records to the training data increased the average F1-score of the classifiers by 1.1 percentage points, the largest gain among the generators compared, and \system was the only generator whose records never lowered the F1-score of any dataset by more than one point.
 \end{abstract}
 
